DESCRIPTION:At a Death Cafe people\, often strangers\, gather to eat sweet
 s and drink tea or coffee and discuss death. Join us for a welcoming discu
 ssion group where people gather to talk openly about death. This is not a 
 grief support or counselling session\, but a space for meaningful\, though
 tful conversations that many participants find surprisingly profound.\nHos
 ted by Jacqueline\;\nShe is passionate about creating spaces for honest co
 nversations about end-of-life awareness. With 20+ years of experience as a
 n Educator\, Holistic Coach\, and Reiki Master\, she supports individuals 
 and families in approaching end-of-life preparation with awareness\, compa
 ssion\, and grace.\nRegistration is welcome but optional.
LOCATION:S. Walter Stewart\, Meeting Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69b4516db48451fc3c08af2f
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Computers for Beginners: Use Gmail
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260610T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260610T153000
UID:69dd523fb6c4ac1fedcc2101@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Computers for Beginners is a 6-week course designed to build y
 our confidence by introducing new skills slowly in a small\, supportive co
 mmunity. At the end of the course\, you may not know everything about the 
 internet\, but you will have a solid foundation of new skills and the conf
 idence to continue learning.\nIs This Course Right for You?\nFor seniors a
 nd beginners\; those with very limited or no computer experience\n6 weeks\
 n: with 1 class per week\nWhat Will You Learn?\nBasic tools such as the mo
 use and the keyboard\nHow to search online for information\, services and 
 entertainment\nHow to use email to keep in touch with family and friends\n
 Week 1:\nUse the Mouse (May 6)\nWeek 2:\nUse the Keyboard (May 13)\nWeek 3
 :\nUse Windows (May 20)\nWeek 4:\nUse the Internet (May 27)\nWeek 5:\nUse 
 Google/Google Maps (June 3)\nWeek 6:\nUse Gmail (June 10)\nLearn to use Gm
 ail\, Google’s free web-based email service.\nIn this last session you w
 ill:\nPractice basic email functions: send\, read\, reply\, forward\, dele
 te email\, and attach files\nReview how spam and junk mail work\nUpon comp
 letion of this class students will have a basic understanding of how to us
 e email.\nInstructions on how to set up and manage your own free Gmail acc
 ount are included in the handout.\nPlease read the following information c
 arefully:\nTo register for the\nWeek 1: Use the Mouse\nyou will need to ei
 ther register\nin person at the Bloor/Gladstone Library\n\, or call us at\
 n416-393-7674\n. This first class is offline registration because not ever
 yone knows how to use the computer mouse right away. When you are register
 ing online\, you will be registered for all classes from Week 2 - Week 6.\
 nRegistration is required.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal i
 nformation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under t
 he Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informat
 ion will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and ev
 aluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be 
 directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 
 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Bloor/Gladstone\, Learning Centre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69dd523fb6c4ac1fedcc2101
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Our Fragile Planet: Create Your Own Nature Reserve – Right at Ho
 me!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260626T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260626T190000
UID:69de9310e2a2952aed0ebfbe@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Imagine visiting your favourite park or conservation area – 
 where the sights\, sounds\, and magic of nature fill you with wonder\, pea
 ce and a sense of connection. Now imagine not having to fight traffic and 
 use polluting fuels to get there!\nIn this presentation\, learn how you ca
 n reimagine your current space to bring you that same refreshing and relax
 ing nature vibe at home. Explore the immense potential of natural spaces a
 t home that protect biodiversity and help to mitigate and adapt to climate
  change through careful plant choices\, design\, and maintenance.\nPresent
 ed by Dorte Windmuller\,\na\nDavid Suzuki Foundation's Butterfly Ranger\n\
 ,\nwho is a community leader in teaching and inspiring people to protect b
 ees\, butterflies\, and wildflowers. This program is part of TPL’s envi
 ronmental program series\,\nOur Fragile Planet\n\, supported by Friends of
  Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.​\nInformation Collection Notice
 :\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Publi
 c Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20
 (d). The information will only be used for event and program registration\
 , delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your in
 formation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 41
 6-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Room 201\, 2nd Floor.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69de9310e2a2952aed0ebfbe
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
